<h1>cycle</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="path.html"><em>path</em></a> that starts and ends at the same <a href="vertex.html"><em>vertex</em></a> and includes at least one <a href="edge.html"><em>edge</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Generalization</strong> (I am a kind of ...)<br>
<a href="path.html"><em>path</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Specialization</strong> (... is a kind of me.)<br>
<a href="hamiltonianCycle.html"><em>Hamiltonian cycle</em></a>, <a href="eulercycle.html"><em>Euler cycle</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>Aggregate parent</strong> (I am a part of or used in ...)<br>
<a href="graph.html"><em>graph</em></a>.
</p>
<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="circuit.html"><em>circuit</em></a> (2).
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Also known as "circuit" or "closed path". </p> 
<p> A cycle includes vertices other than the start/end at most once. </p> 
<p> Having at least one edge means that there are at least two vertices in the path: the start/end and one other.  It also means the path length is at least one. </p> 
<p> One way to find a cycle is to do a <a href="depthfirst.html"><em>depth-first search</em></a>, checking for repeated vertices.  One step in finding all cycles is to look for <a href="stronglyConnectedCompo.html"><em>strongly connected components</em></a>.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>Implementation</h2>
